Starting a solo practice is an exercise in structured uncertainty. You know roughly where you want to end up, but the path there is mostly improvisation with guardrails.

## The First Decisions

Every business starts with a handful of choices that cascade into everything else. For me, those were:

1. **Who do I serve?** Not "everyone who needs marketing" but a specific slice of the market where my experience creates outsized value.
2. **What do I actually deliver?** Not a menu of services but a clear, opinionated methodology.
3. **How do I want to work?** This one matters more than people think. The structure of your days shapes the quality of your output.

## What I'd Do Differently

If I were starting over, I'd spend less time on infrastructure and more time on the first three conversations. The business doesn't exist in your systems — it exists in the space between you and the people you serve.

I'd also be more comfortable with imperfection. The website doesn't need to be perfect on day one. The proposal template doesn't need to cover every edge case. Ship it, learn from the response, and iterate.

## The Trade-Offs Nobody Talks About

Solo work gives you freedom and focus. It also gives you every problem. You're the strategist, the executor, the accountant, and the IT department. The trick is knowing which hats to wear and which to delegate — even when delegation feels expensive.
